+# Block placement: review of the suggested optimizations
+
+Files reviewed: `src/ServerScriptService/BlockPlacement.server.luau` and
+`src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/BlockPlacer.luau`.
+
+## The workload
+
+Before looking at any single item, it helps to know how much work this system does:
+
+- The server accepts at most 10 placements per player per second (`MIN_INTERVAL = 0.1`).
+- A server holds 8 players, so the worst case is **80 placements per second per server**. Real
+  play is far below that: people don't click 10 times a second for long.
+- Each placement is one remote call, a few table lookups, one distance check, and one new
+  anchored Part.
+- The client does one raycast per click or tap.
+- Nobody has reported lag, and there is no profile pointing at this code.
+
+At this rate the whole system costs a few hundredths of a millisecond per second on the server.
+The Luau work in the handler costs microseconds per call. The real cost is the Part itself:
+creating it, replicating it, and rendering it. None of the six suggestions reduces that cost.
+Each one would add code that has to be maintained, and some would change behavior.
+
+The rule this review follows: with no measured problem and a workload this small, keep the
+plain, readable version. Only make a change if it also makes the code simpler or more correct.
+
+## Verdicts
+
+| # | Suggestion | Verdict |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Pool the block Parts | **No** |
+| 2 | Pack PlaceBlock's arguments into a buffer | **No** |
+| 3 | Make PlaceBlock an UnreliableRemoteEvent | **No, and it would introduce a bug** |
+| 4 | `--!native` on both scripts | **No** |
+| 5 | Lookup table instead of `table.find` | **Done**, but as a cleanup, not for speed |
+| 6 | Create RaycastParams once | **No** (harmless, but not worth the extra state) |
+
+### 1. Pool the block Parts: no
+
+A pool pays off when objects are created and destroyed over and over, so that destroyed ones can
+be reused. Placed blocks are never destroyed: the code has no removal path at all. A pool would
+have nothing to recycle. It would only pre-create Parts that sit unused in memory.
+
+Even if removal is added later, pooling is only worth it once a profile shows instance creation
+or garbage collection in spike frames. At most 80 Parts a second per server will not show up
+there. A pooled Part also brings back old state (attributes such as `PlacedBy`, color, material)
+unless every field is reset, which is a new way to introduce bugs.
+
+### 2. Pack the arguments into a buffer: no
+
+Each call sends a short string (for example `"Brick"`) and a `Vector3`. Counting the remote's own
+overhead of about 9 bytes, that is roughly 30 bytes per call. At the 10-per-second cap, that is
+about **0.3 KB/s per player**, a tiny share of a normal client's bandwidth. A buffer might save
+a dozen bytes per call. Nobody would notice the difference.
+
+It would cost a lot in exchange. The server handler currently checks typed values (`typeof`,
+the NaN check, a name lookup). With a buffer, it would first have to check the buffer's length
+and decode it by hand before running those same checks. More code that parses untrusted client
+input means more room for bugs. Buffers save bytes, not time. They are worth using for large or
+frequent payloads, such as per-frame state for many entities, and this is neither.
+
+### 3. Switch to an UnreliableRemoteEvent: no, this would be a bug
+
+Unreliable remotes are not "faster remotes". They skip resending and ordering, so packets can be
+**dropped** or arrive **out of order**. That makes them a good fit for data where the next update
+replaces the last one, such as a position sent every frame. A block placement is a one-time
+action. If the packet is lost, the player's click does nothing and nobody is told why.
+
+Also:
+
+- When a client sends too many calls, unreliable calls get dropped, while reliable ones are only
+  delayed.
+- An unreliable event that fires before the server connects its handler is dropped right away.
+- Delivery time is about the same for both kinds on a healthy connection. Unreliable only helps
+  when a lost packet would otherwise hold up later traffic, and that only matters for constant
+  streams.
+
+PlaceBlock should stay a reliable `RemoteEvent`.
+
+### 4. `--!native` on both scripts: no
+
+Native code generation speeds up pure Luau computation: math-heavy loops, buffer work, number
+crunching. Both scripts mostly call engine APIs (`Instance.new`, property assignments,
+`Workspace:Raycast`, `FireServer`, `SetAttribute`). Native mode does not make those calls
+faster. The Luau part of each call runs in microseconds and only a few times a second. Native
+compilation also uses extra memory for the compiled code. With nothing to speed up, it only
+costs something.
+
+### 5. Lookup table instead of `table.find`: done, as a cleanup
+
+As a speed fix, this doesn't matter. `table.find` over 5 block names takes a few dozen
+nanoseconds. The gap between a list scan and a set lookup only shows up with hundreds or
+thousands of items scanned in a hot loop.
+
+It was still worth changing, for a different reason: the lookup table already existed.
+`BlockTypes.ByName` is built in the shared module, and the server read it on the very next line.
+`VALID_NAMES` was a second copy of the same list, built at startup just to be scanned. The handler
+now looks the name up in `BlockTypes.ByName` once and returns early on `nil`. That removes the
+duplicate list and its setup loop. Behavior is the same: a name passes exactly when it is a key
+in `ByName`, which is the same set of names that was in `VALID_NAMES`.
+
+### 6. Create RaycastParams once: no
+
+Reusing a `RaycastParams` makes sense for raycasts that run every frame. Here, one is created per
+click or tap, a few times a second at most, and that cost is too small to measure.
+
+Caching it would also add state to keep up to date. The filter excludes the player's
+**current** character, and the character is replaced on every respawn. A cached params object
+would need a `CharacterAdded` handler to refresh its filter. If that handler were ever missing or
+wrong, the ray would hit the player's own new body. The current code gets this right without
+extra effort, because it reads `Players.LocalPlayer.Character` at click time. Leave it as is.
+If placement ever becomes continuous (a drag-to-build mode or a per-frame preview ghost), that
+would be the moment to cache it.
+
+## What would actually matter later
+
+If the game grows, the costs worth watching are not in these scripts' Luau, but in the
+**number of placed blocks**:
+
+- Every block is a replicated Part, so total block count drives server memory, join time
+  (new players download every block), and client rendering. Watch `Stats.InstanceCount` and
+  join time once worlds hold thousands of blocks.
+- `occupied` and the `Blocks` folder only grow. A per-player or per-server block cap, or block
+  removal, will matter long before any of the six items above.
+
+If lag is ever reported, start with a MicroProfiler capture and the Developer Console's network
+numbers on the affected machine. Find which subsystem is over budget before changing code.
